| c-chart | 11 | 19.000 | 0.000 | 14.937 |
| u-chart | 11 | 25.333 | 0.959 | 14.641 |
| p-chart | 11 | 0.290 | 0.001 | 0.167 |
| np-chart | 11 | 29.000 | 0.000 | 16.504 |
| Laney p′ chart | 11 | 0.290 | 0.060 | 0.108 |
| Laney u′ chart | 11 | 25.333 | 0.000 | 17.598 |
\image(figures/attribute_charts.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Six charts display inspection statistics and fixed-baseline limits. Variable-denominator charts have a limit for each inspection.)
\alttext(Panels show the c, u, np, p, Laney p-prime and Laney u-prime charts, with limit violations marked in red.)

> Interpret the chart

The u-chart divides the defect count by exposure, allowing fractional exposure
such as inspected area. The p-chart divides the defective count by units
inspected. The p and np panels treat the same defective count sequence as two
inspection designs, one with recorded varying sample sizes and one with exactly
100 units each. The p and u centers pool baseline numerators and denominators.
Compare the regular and Laney limits. The Laney scale comes from adjacent
standardized baseline residuals, so its limits can widen or narrow relative to
the usual model. The red points apply the single-point limit rule.

> References

- [NIST Dataplot control-chart reference|https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/software/dataplot/refman1/auxillar/contchar.htm]
- [Laney, Improved Control Charts for Attributes|https://doi.org/10.1081/QEN-120003555]
